sysl.regex — a tree becomes a program
=====================================

    module sysl.regex

    import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}

Every regular expression construct is a small fixed arrangement of five instructions, and that is
the whole of this file. The tree is walked once, each node emits its arrangement, and what comes out
is a flat program the matcher runs.


The instruction set
-------------------

Nine instructions, of which only three look at the input. The other six move the program counter or
write down a position, which is what makes the matcher's inner loop small: a step either consumes a
character or it does not, and everything that does not is resolved before the character is read.

The three operands that were `arg1` and `arg2` in the engine this is modelled on are named here
instead, because they meant different things per opcode — a target, a slot, a class index — and a
reader had to know which. A bracket expression rides on the instruction rather than in a side array
addressed by index, so the side array and the second kind of index are both gone.

    enum Inst
        MatchChar(ch: char)
        MatchAny
        MatchSet(of: CharClass)
        Accept
        Jump(to: usize)
        Split(first: usize, second: usize)
        Save(slot: usize)
        AssertStart
        AssertEnd
    end Inst

`Split` is the whole of the nondeterminism. It says "both of these are live", and the matcher
follows each — which is why a pattern that would send a backtracking engine exponential costs this
one nothing: the two branches are two threads at two program counters, not two searches.


What the compiler produces
--------------------------

A program plus the number of capture groups it writes. Group `k` owns slots `2k` and `2k+1`, and
group 0 is the whole match — so a pattern with two groups needs six slots.

How long a program is allowed to get. It is a bound on the *pattern*, not on anything a match does,
and it exists because intervals compose multiplicatively: `a{200}{200}{200}` is sixteen characters
and eight million instructions, and one more factor is a billion. Matching being bounded says nothing
about that, so an engine that can be handed a pattern from outside needs the other end bounded too.

A hundred thousand is far past any pattern written on purpose — the whole POSIX grammar of a
real-world pattern is a few dozen — and small enough that the worst case is a few megabytes.

**It is a `u32` rather than the `usize` a length is**, because the limit is a property of the
*grammar* rather than of the machine. A 16-bit target cannot name a hundred thousand at all, so a
`usize` here made this file refuse to compile for one — and lowering the number to what such a
machine can hold would weaken the bound everywhere else to describe a target the pattern can never
reach anyway. Widening the length to ask the question costs nothing and says the true thing: where
the whole address space is 64 KiB the limit is unreachable, and the allocator gives out first.

    const max_program: u32 = 100000

    struct Program
        code: &Buf[Inst]
        groups: int

        // Set when a program ran past the limit. Everything after that point is not emitted and the
        // walk stops descending, so the cost of refusing is paid once rather than per node.
        over: bool

        len(self) -> usize = self.code.len()

        at(self, pc: usize) -> Inst = self.code.at(pc)

        // How many capture positions a run of this program has to carry.
        slots(self) -> usize = usize(self.groups + 1) * 2

        // Append an instruction and answer where it landed, which is what every jump target below
        // is derived from.
        emit(*self, i: Inst) -> usize
            if u32(self.code.len()) >= max_program
                self.over = true
                return 0

            var pc = self.code.len()

            self.code.push(i)
            pc
        end emit

        // Overwrite an instruction already emitted. A forward branch is emitted before its target
        // exists, so it goes out with placeholder operands and is corrected once the code it jumps
        // over has been laid down.
        patch(*self, pc: usize, i: Inst)
            self.code.set(pc, i)


The arrangements
----------------

Each is written as a listing with the labels the code below computes, so that the two can be read
against one another.

A literal, a dot, an anchor or a bracket expression is one instruction. Concatenation is the two
sides in order and no instruction of its own. The rest:

```
e*          L1: split L2, L3        e+      L1: e
            L2: e                           L2: split L1, L3
                jump L1                 L3:
            L3:

e?              split L1, L2        a|b         split L1, L2
            L1: e                           L1: a
            L2:                                 jump L3
                                            L2: b
                                            L3:

(e) as group k  save 2k
                e
                save 2k+1
```

`e*` prefers the branch that matches, and `e?` prefers the branch that takes `e` — the greedy
reading in both cases. Preference decides nothing on its own here, because the matcher runs every
branch; it decides which thread is already at a program counter when a second one arrives there,
which is what makes the answer the longest one rather than merely a matching one.

        private walk(*self, n: &Node)
            if self.over then return

            *n match
                Empty ->
                    return
                Lit(c) ->
                    self.emit(MatchChar(c))
                AnyChar ->
                    self.emit(MatchAny)
                AtStart ->
                    self.emit(AssertStart)
                AtEnd ->
                    self.emit(AssertEnd)
                InSet(cls) ->
                    self.emit(MatchSet(cls))
                Seq(l, r) ->
                    self.walk(l)
                    self.walk(r)
                Star(inner) ->
                    var l1 = self.emit(Split(0, 0))

                    self.walk(inner)
                    self.emit(Jump(l1))

                    var l3 = self.len()

                    self.patch(l1, Split(l1 + 1, l3))
                Plus(inner) ->
                    var l1 = self.len()

                    self.walk(inner)

                    var l3 = self.len() + 1

                    self.emit(Split(l1, l3))
                Quest(inner) ->
                    var split = self.emit(Split(0, 0))

                    self.walk(inner)

                    var l2 = self.len()

                    self.patch(split, Split(split + 1, l2))
                Alt(l, r) ->
                    var split = self.emit(Split(0, 0))

                    self.walk(l)

                    var jmp = self.emit(Jump(0))
                    var l2 = self.len()

                    self.walk(r)

                    var l3 = self.len()

                    self.patch(split, Split(split + 1, l2))
                    self.patch(jmp, Jump(l3))
                Group(inner, k) ->
                    var slot = usize(k) * 2

                    self.emit(Save(slot))
                    self.walk(inner)
                    self.emit(Save(slot + 1))
        end walk
    end Program

The whole pattern is wrapped in group 0's pair of `Save`s, so the matcher needs no separate notion
of where the match began and ended: the span of the whole match is the span of a capture group like
any other, and reporting it is reading slots 0 and 1.

    compile(root: &Node, groups: int) -> Result[Program, RegexError]
        var p = Program(buf(), groups, false)

        p.emit(Save(0))
        p.walk(root)
        p.emit(Save(1))
        p.emit(Accept)

        if p.over then Err(PatternTooLarge) else Ok(p)
    end compile

    compile_pattern(pattern: string) -> Result[Program, RegexError]
        var t = parse_counted(pattern)?

        compile(t.0, t.1)
    end compile_pattern


Reading a program back
----------------------

The listing, one instruction to a line with its program counter. It exists for the same reason
`show` does — a caller asking what their pattern actually became — and it is what the compiler is
checked against, because a mis-computed jump target is invisible in the answers a small pattern
gives and obvious here.

    dump(p: Program) -> string
        var out = ""

        for pc in 0..<p.len()
            out += s"$pc: ${show_inst(p.at(pc))}\n"

        out
    end dump

A bracket expression is rendered by handing it back to `show` as the node it came from, rather than
by a renderer of its own here. That is not only shorter: it is what makes the listing and the tree
agree about a class by construction, so a reader comparing the two is reading one piece of code
twice.

    private show_inst(i: Inst) -> string = i match
        MatchChar(c) -> s"char $c"
        MatchAny -> "any"
        MatchSet(cls) -> show(InSet(cls))
        Accept -> "accept"
        Jump(to) -> s"jump $to"
        Split(a, b) -> s"split $a, $b"
        Save(slot) -> s"save $slot"
        AssertStart -> "start"
        AssertEnd -> "end"
    end show_inst
